WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:09.230
Hello and welcome back to
another episode with, ItsDrDan.

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:13.779
Today we're going to be learning
how to convert D and L glucose from

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:15.819
a fissure to a Haworth projection.

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:20.749
Now, what makes this so different than my
previous videos on the Haworth projections

00:00:21.199 --> 00:00:26.170
is that D and L are enantiomers of
one another, meaning that the Haworth

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:30.780
projection is going to end up being
flipped because of that enantiomer shape.

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:34.180
So we're going to go through this
step by step and show exactly

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:36.210
how to do this for glucose.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:43.970
First thing that we're going to
do is have our Fischer projection

00:00:44.249 --> 00:00:47.480
and I have the OHs here labeled in
red so you can see the difference

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:48.770
and make it a little bit easier.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.570
So we're going to first start
by drawing the enantiomer.

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:57.230
Now when we go through this and draw
that enantiomer, remember that entire

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:03.330
thing, the way to look at that is This
is a mirror image or reflection, right?

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:04.440
So it's just like yourself.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.800
You're looking in the mirror
and you're waving at yourself.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:12.050
So the first thing that I would do is
I would redraw that general backbone

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:17.219
just like how we have before and the
things that are going to be mirrored

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:22.850
that are the important parts are the OH's
because they are on those chiral carbons.

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:23.490
Right?

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:26.750
So we have a chiral carbon
here, here, here, and here.

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:29.470
Those are all shown by
those different crosses.

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:33.410
So when we are writing that we
are going to fill in, I always

00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:35.490
like to start with the OHs.

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:40.089
So that way you're, you're just
going to fill in the important

00:01:40.089 --> 00:01:43.789
functional group and each one of
these is paired with a hydrogen.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.149
So there's a hydrogen on one side.

00:01:45.565 --> 00:01:46.505
Oh, H on the other.

00:01:47.115 --> 00:01:50.455
So I'll write down maybe like one
side, so like on the left I have

00:01:50.455 --> 00:01:55.875
OH, H, OH, OH, and then I'll write
all the pairs on the right side.

00:01:56.265 --> 00:01:56.914
Just like so.

00:01:57.764 --> 00:01:59.205
So we have our enantiomer.

00:01:59.595 --> 00:02:01.395
Now, what exactly does that mean?

00:02:01.395 --> 00:02:08.634
So that means on the right side I now
have L glucose for the other structure.

00:02:09.014 --> 00:02:11.264
How can you tell if it's L or D?

00:02:11.645 --> 00:02:16.625
Well, the way to remember if it's L or
D has to do with the last chiral carbon.

00:02:17.075 --> 00:02:19.815
So if we kind of go through
these, right, each one of these

00:02:19.815 --> 00:02:21.595
here, like I said, are chiral.

00:02:22.135 --> 00:02:25.295
And what we're going to do is we're
going to look at the last one in the

00:02:25.295 --> 00:02:29.644
line, so the one I put a little red
star next to, this is the last one.

00:02:30.355 --> 00:02:34.605
And what we can see is, well, on the,
on the left structure, the OH is on the

00:02:34.605 --> 00:02:37.894
right side, so for D, um, so for dexter.

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:42.929
So D is on, on the right, and
that is going to be our right RD.

00:02:43.559 --> 00:02:46.820
Now on the bottom one,
we have L is on the left.

00:02:47.149 --> 00:02:52.299
So L here, we will denote that with
the letter L to say that this is left.

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:53.809
So D and L.

00:02:54.389 --> 00:02:56.419
Now let's go on to our next step.

00:02:56.599 --> 00:02:57.120
All right.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:01.000
So our next step is all about
rotating the Fisher projection.

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:04.800
So for this, I already went
through the process of rotating it.

00:03:04.839 --> 00:03:08.380
What we will do is we're
going to rotate it 90 degrees.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:10.200
to the right.

00:03:10.619 --> 00:03:15.570
Now the reason why we are doing this is
so that it helps us see a couple things.

00:03:16.109 --> 00:03:20.949
The first one is we want to make sure
that we keep all of our carbons numbered.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:25.539
It's really important to see where
all your OHs are going to be going

00:03:25.799 --> 00:03:29.629
because it matters if they're top
or bottom on your final structure.

00:03:30.295 --> 00:03:37.855
So we have 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, so we
have all those individual numbers.

00:03:38.565 --> 00:03:42.775
Now, what is also important about
top and bottom here is what I mean

00:03:42.775 --> 00:03:46.204
is, notice how we have some OHs
on the bottom of our structure.

00:03:46.684 --> 00:03:51.395
So if we kind of imagine a little,
a little border line that we have

00:03:51.395 --> 00:03:55.385
here, we have some that are on the
bottom, some that are on the top.

00:03:55.935 --> 00:03:57.865
So what we'll do is we will label that.

00:03:57.875 --> 00:03:59.154
So this is going to be top.

00:03:59.704 --> 00:04:02.024
And in green we are going to have bottom.

00:04:03.015 --> 00:04:07.385
And it's going to be the same idea too
for the other structure or the enantiomer.

00:04:07.395 --> 00:04:10.864
So for D and L we will keep our same idea.

00:04:10.864 --> 00:04:18.240
We'll number it as well 1, 2, 3, And we
will also have our top and bottom for L.

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:24.210
And you can see already that both
of these are flipped as a result

00:04:24.299 --> 00:04:26.619
of R being a different isomer.

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:30.919
So all the OHs for the L one are
all going to be pointing, most of

00:04:30.919 --> 00:04:31.979
them are going to be pointing up.

00:04:32.325 --> 00:04:34.675
Or as you can see for D, most
of them are pointing down.

00:04:34.724 --> 00:04:37.065
So it's going to be a really
big difference in our structure.

00:04:37.775 --> 00:04:39.025
Let's look at the next step.

00:04:39.225 --> 00:04:43.715
For step 3, what makes this so different
is that we need to be able to figure

00:04:43.715 --> 00:04:46.085
out how big is our ring going to be.

00:04:46.564 --> 00:04:52.955
Most monosaccharides, or simple sugars,
are generally a 5 or a 6 member ring.

00:04:53.470 --> 00:04:58.580
meaning it's going to be one of these
two shapes that I have written down here.

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:02.310
Now, the one thing that's different
about the numbering here is

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:07.310
notice that I have a number on
our ox, on an oxygen on the ring.

00:05:07.899 --> 00:05:11.589
These are both considered
to be heterocyclic rings.

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:12.080
Okay.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:14.900
Being that hetero meaning different.

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:22.580
So what makes it heterocyclic is that it
has a, uh, oxygen embedded in the ring.

00:05:23.375 --> 00:05:26.135
So whenever we have a
heterocyclic species, that means

00:05:26.135 --> 00:05:27.095
we have something different.

00:05:27.635 --> 00:05:31.795
So when we are going through these,
we're going to have to number them.

00:05:31.995 --> 00:05:34.844
Before I had it just by
numbering the carbons, right?

00:05:34.845 --> 00:05:36.444
We went, we started the carbonyl.

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:37.990
We numbered all the way down.

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:44.500
We're gonna do the same idea, but this is
what I always call an unofficial numbering

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:51.220
because alls we wanna know is how big
our ring is when we go through this.

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:54.640
So once again, we're gonna
start at the C double bond O.

00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:59.710
So always at the carbon yield
is your first number, regardless

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:02.050
of if it's an Aldo or a keto.

00:06:02.290 --> 00:06:06.430
We are going to start at this
one for just how big is the ring?

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:14.450
So we have 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and then we're
going to that last chiral carbon, so

00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:20.480
we're gonna put a little red star next to
it again, and our last numbered spot is

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:27.460
going to be the oxygen that is on the last
alcohol, alright, so that's gonna be 6.

00:06:28.190 --> 00:06:32.210
meaning that this structure is
going to have the same shape

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:33.929
as the one on the top left.

00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:39.809
So we will have our six member ring,
just like so, and it's going to have

00:06:39.809 --> 00:06:43.610
an OH on the structure, and it's
going to look something like this.

00:06:43.899 --> 00:06:45.879
And we can redraw the numbering on here.

00:06:45.879 --> 00:06:47.709
It's going to be one, two, three.

00:06:48.310 --> 00:06:50.099
4, 5, 6.

00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:56.390
And that 6 oxygen that's here on this
ring comes from this chiral spot.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:59.349
And if you came here to figure
out mechanisms, go to my

00:06:59.349 --> 00:07:01.219
hemiacetal and acetal video.

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:03.499
I will go through the
mechanism of how to do this.

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:06.630
This is more how to draw
Haworth projections quickly.

00:07:07.869 --> 00:07:10.179
So now it's the same idea
for this other structure.

00:07:10.179 --> 00:07:11.449
It's going to be the same thing.

00:07:11.450 --> 00:07:16.499
Being that they're both enantiomers, their
skeletal structure It's still the same.

00:07:16.499 --> 00:07:18.789
It's the chirality is the different part.

00:07:18.789 --> 00:07:20.929
So we're only putting the ring here.

00:07:21.070 --> 00:07:23.330
It's only the carbons
that we're interested in.

00:07:23.729 --> 00:07:28.099
So both of these structures are
going to be that six member ring.

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:31.810
So let's go on to our next step here.

00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:35.530
Step four is all about where
to place all of your OH groups.

00:07:35.960 --> 00:07:39.629
So previously I mentioned that it's
very important that we go through

00:07:39.629 --> 00:07:43.669
and number our rings because we need
to make sure that we are placing

00:07:43.689 --> 00:07:45.119
everything in the right spot.

00:07:45.589 --> 00:07:47.619
So let's first start with the OH groups.

00:07:48.129 --> 00:07:51.340
So if we notice we have this general
shape, so we have our hemiacetal

00:07:51.989 --> 00:07:57.299
structure, we have our anomeric carbon
that is on number one, so we'll talk

00:07:57.310 --> 00:07:59.359
about the anomeric carbon in a little bit.

00:07:59.789 --> 00:08:02.950
That's just, we're just going to
place that facing up in the meantime.

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:08.510
So, now, for our next groups, we're
going to put in red all of our OH groups.

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:11.010
So we're going to go
from carbon to carbon.

00:08:11.010 --> 00:08:15.210
So carbon one was the aldehyde
that turned into an alcohol.

00:08:16.010 --> 00:08:19.350
Now carbon two, the OH
is pointing down, right?

00:08:19.350 --> 00:08:24.060
So being that we see that downward, we're
going to fill that in going down as well.

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:26.350
We're now going to move to carbon three.

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:30.160
Carbon three, we see that
it is on the top side.

00:08:30.670 --> 00:08:34.095
So being that this is top, We
are going to go through and

00:08:34.095 --> 00:08:35.745
we're going to put it going up.

00:08:36.645 --> 00:08:40.185
Now carbon 4, we see that
it is going down, so we're

00:08:40.185 --> 00:08:41.585
going to fill that in as well.

00:08:42.305 --> 00:08:44.835
Now carbon 5 is different
than all the others.

00:08:45.084 --> 00:08:46.295
Now why is that?

00:08:46.645 --> 00:08:49.415
Well, if you remember
from your reactions here.

00:08:49.945 --> 00:08:55.615
This particular carbon this one
is where this OH is what's doing

00:08:55.615 --> 00:09:00.405
the bonding So that OH there let's
color code that for ourselves.

00:09:00.825 --> 00:09:04.755
So this OH let's make it we'll
make it Um, how about green?

00:09:05.634 --> 00:09:10.035
So being that we have our green O
that one is what is going to become

00:09:10.065 --> 00:09:16.275
a part of the ring structure itself
So something happens here when this

00:09:16.385 --> 00:09:22.765
chain Opened or right before it closed,
it goes to a process known as Muta

00:09:22.795 --> 00:09:30.074
rotation, which can cause this last
TH two group here that's on carbon

00:09:30.074 --> 00:09:34.015
number six to rotate either up or down.

00:09:34.665 --> 00:09:36.555
Now, how do you know where it's gonna go?

00:09:36.585 --> 00:09:38.085
Because we have six carbons.

00:09:38.725 --> 00:09:39.685
So on Carbon five.

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:47.040
We have a c H two oh group that's going to
be pointing either up or down, and it has

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:49.350
to do with whether or not it was D or L.

00:09:49.890 --> 00:09:53.210
So if it was D, it's
going to be pointing up.

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:55.690
If it was L, it's gonna be pointing down.

00:09:57.060 --> 00:10:02.010
We're gonna put that in, so we have
a c H two, and it's going to be

00:10:02.280 --> 00:10:05.030
pointing up, and we can see that here.

00:10:05.870 --> 00:10:08.660
Now what's the difference between
what we were doing before?

00:10:09.150 --> 00:10:12.000
Well, if we go to the
other structure for L.

00:10:12.445 --> 00:10:13.755
So that's down here.

00:10:14.265 --> 00:10:15.835
How are they going to compare?

00:10:16.475 --> 00:10:19.195
Well, L, where we're going, once again,
we're going to go through the entire

00:10:19.195 --> 00:10:21.355
process of filling everything in.

00:10:21.975 --> 00:10:27.360
So if we start at carbon number one, So
let's go through and renumber everything.

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:34.620
So we got 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and
then we have 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.

00:10:34.709 --> 00:10:36.360
And then carbon number six.

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:38.220
We're numbering our carbons this time.

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:39.480
'cause we already know our ring size.

00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:40.470
So official numbering.

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:44.610
So the first thing is, where
does the CH two oh H go?

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:45.060
Let's go.

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:45.870
Let's start with that one.

00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:49.569
Like how we did last time while being
that it is L, it's this time it's

00:10:49.569 --> 00:10:51.969
gonna be pointing down as a result.

00:10:52.450 --> 00:10:57.380
So what we're going to do is in our
structure, We are going to have a

00:10:57.390 --> 00:11:03.930
CH2OH inside, like so, pointing down,
and we are going to write it like so.

00:11:04.449 --> 00:11:08.500
And here, let's make this ink size
a little bit smaller, so that way

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:10.220
we can see it a little better.

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:14.259
So CH2OH, alright?

00:11:14.729 --> 00:11:18.099
So that's pointing down
on carbon number five.

00:11:18.830 --> 00:11:22.250
Now for the other carbons, what
is going to be so different is

00:11:22.250 --> 00:11:23.440
we're going to do the same thing.

00:11:24.055 --> 00:11:27.925
We're going to go through this, and
we're going to put our OHs pointing

00:11:27.975 --> 00:11:30.055
up and down, going carbon to carbon.

00:11:30.685 --> 00:11:32.354
So carbon 2, we have up.

00:11:32.854 --> 00:11:34.055
Carbon 3 is down.

00:11:34.435 --> 00:11:35.464
Carbon 4 is up.

00:11:35.545 --> 00:11:36.755
So up, down, up.

00:11:36.774 --> 00:11:38.615
It's like kind of having
a cheat code in a game.

00:11:39.685 --> 00:11:45.544
So we have up, down, and then up.

00:11:46.885 --> 00:11:49.094
And that is what our
structure would look like.

00:11:49.395 --> 00:11:54.945
Now comparing the two, so if I
bring the other one down, We can

00:11:54.955 --> 00:11:59.705
see that the OH groups are flipped
between the different structures.

00:12:00.095 --> 00:12:03.904
Notice how the top one is, uh, if
we go through here, we see down,

00:12:03.965 --> 00:12:07.274
up, down between 2, 3, and 4.

00:12:07.655 --> 00:12:11.105
And on the other structure,
it's up, down, up.

00:12:11.664 --> 00:12:16.835
So this is the difference between
whenever we have a D structure and

00:12:16.835 --> 00:12:20.615
an L structure for these two pieces.

00:12:21.280 --> 00:12:25.220
Let's go on to the last little
bit about alpha and beta anomers.

00:12:25.700 --> 00:12:31.379
Step five in the final step is all
about alpha and beta anomers, which

00:12:31.379 --> 00:12:35.710
tends to be one of the most confusing
parts for going over carbohydrates.

00:12:36.259 --> 00:12:37.390
Now why is that?

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:41.700
Well mainly it's because both
alpha and beta can exist.

00:12:42.140 --> 00:12:44.020
Now what is an alpha or a beta?

00:12:44.540 --> 00:12:48.120
Well it's all about looking
at the anomeric carbon.

00:12:48.630 --> 00:12:56.379
So, for all of these, the anomeric carbon
is wherever we have a hemiacetal, meaning

00:12:56.430 --> 00:13:01.270
that we are looking for an ether and
an alcohol connected to the same spot.

00:13:01.814 --> 00:13:05.875
Or, on a disaccharide, this could
be also the acetal or the ketal.

00:13:06.915 --> 00:13:10.564
So, on this structure, the one
thing that we are seeing is, it's

00:13:10.574 --> 00:13:15.854
all about where are the CH2 and
the CH2OH, where are they facing?

00:13:16.525 --> 00:13:19.634
So we are always looking at two areas.

00:13:20.045 --> 00:13:25.954
So if I have, right down below, I
have both D glucose and L glucose.

00:13:26.710 --> 00:13:30.810
In order to tell if something
is beta, so beta is all about,

00:13:31.180 --> 00:13:35.310
beta is cis, alpha is trans.

00:13:36.430 --> 00:13:40.119
So what exactly are we referring to
with cis and trans, because usually

00:13:40.119 --> 00:13:41.840
people think about double bonds.

00:13:42.610 --> 00:13:50.510
So for something to be cis, the
CH2OH and your anomeric OH must

00:13:50.540 --> 00:13:55.840
either both be pointing up, like a,
like a touchdown or a field goal, or

00:13:55.890 --> 00:13:57.560
they must be both be pointing down.

00:13:58.820 --> 00:14:03.075
Now, on Trans, well, you might be
able to guess it, well, if they're

00:14:03.075 --> 00:14:07.115
both pointing down or up together
for beta, or cis, they're the same.

00:14:08.035 --> 00:14:13.285
On trans, it's all about, well, if one
of them is pointing, let's say, up, the

00:14:13.285 --> 00:14:15.854
other one is going to be pointing down.

00:14:16.714 --> 00:14:19.404
Okay, so think of these
kind of like little arrows.

00:14:19.895 --> 00:14:20.735
pointing up, pointing down.

00:14:21.805 --> 00:14:25.375
Now in order for something to be
the opposite, right, for the other

00:14:25.375 --> 00:14:26.855
side, it's the same general thing.

00:14:26.855 --> 00:14:30.285
If one's pointing up, the
other one must be pointing down

00:14:30.295 --> 00:14:32.194
for CH2OH on the other side.

00:14:33.025 --> 00:14:36.684
So if we were to kind of look at
all those structures above, we

00:14:36.685 --> 00:14:43.530
can further classify each of these
individual, uh, rings here that we have.

00:14:44.470 --> 00:14:48.360
Now, if we look at the first one on
the left hand side, what do you see?

00:14:48.620 --> 00:14:54.330
Well, the CH2OH is pointing up and
the OH itself is also pointing up.

00:14:54.809 --> 00:14:59.850
And in fact, we can even draw this so
that way, It is pointing up so that way

00:14:59.870 --> 00:15:03.520
it helps illustrate an up for you, right?

00:15:03.550 --> 00:15:04.910
Because that is the correct way.

00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:11.489
So being that is the case, this
is technically beta D glucose

00:15:11.549 --> 00:15:12.840
because of that rotation.

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:19.500
Now for this other one, you can see that,
oh, well we have an OH that's pointing

00:15:20.865 --> 00:15:26.975
And we have our, our, our CH2OH on the
inside of the ring is pointing down.

00:15:27.515 --> 00:15:30.974
So this is referred to as alpha L glucose.

00:15:32.025 --> 00:15:33.485
How do you know if it's alpha or beta?

00:15:33.485 --> 00:15:34.615
How do you know where this OH went?

00:15:34.625 --> 00:15:36.115
Because I just put it going up.

00:15:36.645 --> 00:15:38.685
It can also be going down as well.

00:15:39.624 --> 00:15:46.084
The reason is, going back up here,
is the ring is able to reopen itself.

00:15:46.505 --> 00:15:48.095
It can open and close.

00:15:48.565 --> 00:15:53.985
Now when this happens, the OH could
rotate where it's either pointing up

00:15:54.475 --> 00:15:56.545
or it's pointing down as a result.

00:15:56.815 --> 00:16:01.415
In fact, with most carbohydrates in
the body, 63 percent of them exist

00:16:01.425 --> 00:16:04.704
as beta, the other 37 act as alpha.

00:16:05.414 --> 00:16:07.575
So now, how do you know
with your problems?

00:16:07.645 --> 00:16:11.205
Most of the time it's generally
specified in homework and exam

00:16:11.215 --> 00:16:13.545
problems which one to draw.

00:16:14.085 --> 00:16:16.084
And if not, they might
ask you to draw both.

00:16:16.635 --> 00:16:19.795
So for this problem, you can see
that we did alpha and beta here.

00:16:20.245 --> 00:16:25.265
So if, let's say if we just copy and paste
this here, how would you draw the other?

00:16:25.355 --> 00:16:27.565
Well, it's all about
just flipping that OH.

00:16:28.645 --> 00:16:36.065
So if we erase here and here, so in
order to do the other, it would just be

00:16:36.075 --> 00:16:38.545
all about, well, before we had beta D.

00:16:40.035 --> 00:16:45.545
So if we make alpha D, we can
see, oh, hey, the OH is down.

00:16:45.545 --> 00:16:50.875
So that's alpha D and then beta
L, you would have them both being

00:16:50.875 --> 00:16:52.475
pointing in the same direction.

00:16:53.125 --> 00:16:57.355
So now they're both pointing as
a result and we can just flip the

00:16:57.355 --> 00:16:59.015
OH and that's all you have to do.

00:16:59.365 --> 00:17:03.665
They both exist naturally in your body
and they both get metabolized different

00:17:03.665 --> 00:17:05.944
ways because of alpha or beta or L or D.

00:17:06.225 --> 00:17:08.735
They all have different purposes for
what they're going to be used for.

